From: gstreamer-custom-build local patches
Subject: [PATCH] mpegpsdemux: do not zero base_time against the upstream segment

On the first pack header, base_time is set to the first SCR - the sender's own
clock - and gst_ps_demux_send_segment() adds it to the segment it pushes, so
that the segment and the buffer timestamps (which come straight from the PES
clock) share one timeline. A block right after that then "adjusts" base_time
against the upstream segment, and it has two defects:

    if (demux->sink_segment.start > demux->base_time)
      diff = demux->sink_segment.start - demux->base_time;
    else
      diff = demux->base_time - demux->sink_segment.start;
    if (abs(diff) > GST_SECOND) {
      demux->base_time -= diff;
    }

First, diff is a gint64 and abs() takes an int, so the value is truncated to
its low 32 bits before the comparison. Whether the block runs at all therefore
depends on bits 0-31 of a nanosecond timestamp, which is to say on nothing:

    base_time 10:56:45.047 -> 39405047488888 ns -> (int) -1277451912 -> runs
    base_time 11:50:19.473 -> 42619473455555 ns -> (int)   512977347 -> skipped

Two live streams from the same sender, differing only in where their clocks
happened to be, take opposite branches. GCC does say so with
-Wabsolute-value, which is not in the build's warning set.

Second, both arms compute a magnitude, and the result is then subtracted
unconditionally. For any live stream - an RTP MPEG-PS feed arriving through
rtpmp2pdepay, where upstream's segment starts at zero and the SCR is a wall
clock - the second arm gives diff == base_time, and the subtraction sets
base_time to exactly zero. The segment then starts at zero while every buffer
still carries the sender's clock, tens of thousands of seconds later. Nothing
downstream can reconcile the two: vacompositor sees frames whose running time
is eleven hours in the future, holds them, and holds their VA surfaces with
them until the decoder's pool has taken the whole GPU.

The adjustment cannot be repaired by fixing the sign, because moving base_time
moves only the segment. Buffer timestamps are assigned from the PES clock in
gst_ps_demux_send_data() and are not touched, so any base_time other than the
one the segment arithmetic already assumes leaves the two disagreeing. Keeping
base_time at the first SCR is what the skipped branch does, and it is the
branch that produces a consistent timeline - so the block goes.

The SCR bookkeeping it sat inside is untouched: first_scr, first_scr_offset and
the initial SCR rate are still recorded, and seeking, which reads base_time
back through first_pts, still sees the value the segment was built from.

--- a/gst/mpegdemux/gstmpegdemux.c
+++ b/gst/mpegdemux/gstmpegdemux.c
@@ -1866,8 +1866,6 @@ gst_ps_demux_parse_pack_start (GstPsDemux * demux)
 
   /* keep the first src in order to calculate delta time */
   if (G_UNLIKELY (demux->first_scr == G_MAXUINT64)) {
-    gint64 diff;
-
     demux->first_scr = scr;
     demux->first_scr_offset = demux->cur_scr_offset;
     demux->base_time = MPEGTIME_TO_GSTTIME (demux->first_scr);
@@ -1876,19 +1874,11 @@ gst_ps_demux_parse_pack_start (GstPsDemux * demux)
     /* at begin consider the new_rate as the scr rate, bytes/clock ticks */
     scr_rate_n = new_rate;
     scr_rate_d = CLOCK_FREQ;
-    /* our SCR timeline might have offset wrt upstream timeline */
-    if (demux->sink_segment.format == GST_FORMAT_TIME) {
-      if (demux->sink_segment.start > demux->base_time)
-        diff = demux->sink_segment.start - demux->base_time;
-      else
-        diff = demux->base_time - demux->sink_segment.start;
-      if (abs(diff) > GST_SECOND) {
-        GST_DEBUG_OBJECT (demux, "diff of %" GST_TIME_FORMAT
-            " wrt upstream start %" GST_TIME_FORMAT "; adjusting base",
-            GST_TIME_ARGS (diff), GST_TIME_ARGS (demux->sink_segment.start));
-	 demux->base_time -= diff;
-      }
-    }
+    /* Local fix: what used to be here moved base_time to the upstream
+     * segment's start, which for a live feed is zero, while leaving the buffer
+     * timestamps on the sender's clock. See the patch header for why it cannot
+     * be corrected in place: base_time has to stay the value the segment
+     * arithmetic in gst_ps_demux_send_segment() is written against. */
   } else if (G_LIKELY (demux->first_scr_offset != demux->cur_scr_offset)) {
     /* estimate byte rate related to the SCR */
     scr_rate_n = demux->cur_scr_offset - demux->first_scr_offset;
